from pathlib import Path
import hashlib,json
out=Path('research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-owner-step3-cellular-branching-bindings-round1/parallel-jm-biadjunction-suppliers')
ids=['def-generalized-jm-primary-idempotents-and-residue-summand-functors','lem-finite-group-induction-is-coinduction-and-thus-biadjoint']
guards={}
for id in ids:
 s=Path('items/'+id+'.md').read_bytes(); (out/(id+'.authentic-preimage.md')).write_bytes(s);guards[id]={'canonical_path':'items/'+id+'.md','raw_sha256':hashlib.sha256(s).hexdigest(),'before_file':str(out/(id+'.authentic-preimage.md'))}
s=(out/(ids[0]+'.authentic-preimage.md')).read_text()
s=s.replace('For a content vector\n$\\alpha\\in(\\mathbb Z/p)^n$ put $I_\\alpha:=\\{\\mathbf i:\n\\mathbf i\\text{ is a permutation of }\\alpha\\}$ and', 'Write $\\mathcal C_n=(\\mathbb F_p)^n/S_n$ for the set of content classes\n(equivalently, multisets of $n$ residues). For $\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_n$ let\n$I_\\alpha$ be the set of sequences representing $\\alpha$, and put')
s=s.replace('and a content vector\n$\\alpha\\in(\\mathbb Z/p)^{n-1}$, put','and a content class\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_{n-1}$, put')
s=s.replace('\\{e_{\\alpha,\\alpha_i}\\}_{i,\\alpha}', '\\{e_{\\alpha,\\alpha_i}\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb F_p,\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_{n-1}}')
s=s.replace('\\sum_{\\alpha\\in(\\mathbb Z/p)^{n-1}}','\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_{n-1}}')
s=s.replace('$\\alpha\\in(\\mathbb Z/p)^d$, i.e.', '$\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_d$, i.e.')
s=s.replace('for a\ngeneral finite-dimensional $M$ put','for a\ngeneral finite-dimensional $M$, with the sum indexed by $\\alpha\\in\\mathcal C_d$, put')
a=s.index('4.1 *Centrality'); b=s.index('\n5.1 ',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''4.1 *An integral central identity.* In $\mathbb ZS_n$ one has $\prod_{r=1}^n(u+L_r)=\sum_{g\in S_n}u^{c(g)}g$, where $c(g)$ is the number of cycles, including fixed points. To prove this by induction, regard $S_{n-1}$ as fixing $n$. A term multiplied by $u$ leaves $n$ fixed and adds one cycle. A term multiplied by $(a,n)$ inserts $n$ in the cycle containing $a$, without changing its cycle count. Conversely, deleting a nonfixed $n$ from its cycle uniquely recovers the permutation in $S_{n-1}$ and the transposition used. Thus every permutation occurs exactly once. Comparing coefficients, each elementary symmetric polynomial $C_s$ in $L_1,\ldots,L_n$ is a sum of all permutations with $n-s$ cycles and hence is central; the identity and centrality remain true after reduction to $k$. [F1, given, algebra]

4.2 *Central CRT separates contents.* Choose $N\ge1$ such that $(L_r-i_r)^Ne(\mathbf i)=0$ for every $r$ and every tuple $\mathbf i$, using step 2.1 and the finiteness of the indices. On the algebra summand with identity $e(\mathbf i)$, the commuting elements $(L_r-i_r)e(\mathbf i)$ generate an ideal whose $D$-th power is zero for $D=n(N-1)+1$: every monomial of degree $D$ has a factor with exponent at least $N$. Thus $C_s-c_s(\mathbf i)$ belongs to that nilpotent ideal, where $c_s(\mathbf i)$ is the elementary symmetric function of the residues. The tuple $c(\mathbf i)=(c_1(\mathbf i),\ldots,c_n(\mathbf i))$ determines the content, because $\prod_r(X-i_r)=\sum_{s=0}^n(-1)^sc_s(\mathbf i)X^{n-s}$ determines the multiplicities of its distinct linear factors, over every characteristic. In $R=k[Y_1,\ldots,Y_n]$, the distinct content tuples therefore define distinct pairwise comaximal maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_\alpha=(Y_s-c_s(\alpha):1\le s\le n)$; their powers $\mathfrak m_\alpha^D$ are also pairwise comaximal, since expanding $1=(a+b)^{2D-1}$ for $a+b=1$ places every term in one of the two $D$-th powers. By [F4], choose $Q_\alpha\in R$ congruent to $1$ modulo $\mathfrak m_\alpha^D$ and to $0$ modulo every other $\mathfrak m_\beta^D$. The nilpotence just proved gives $Q_\alpha(C_1,\ldots,C_n)e(\mathbf i)=e(\mathbf i)$ when $\mathbf i\in I_\alpha$, and $0$ otherwise. Since the tuple idempotents sum to $1$, it follows that $Q_\alpha(C_1,\ldots,C_n)=\sum_{\mathbf i\in I_\alpha}e(\mathbf i)=e_\alpha$. This is central by step 4.1. Idempotency, pairwise orthogonality for distinct content classes, and $\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal C_n}e_\alpha=1$ follow from step 3.1 and the partition of all tuples into their content classes. [F4, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, algebra]
''' +s[b:]
a=s.index('5.1 *The functor'); b=s.index('\n6.1 ',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''5.1 *The functor projectors.* Fix $i\in\mathbb F_p$ and $\alpha\in\mathcal C_{n-1}$. The primary projectors for $L_r$, $r<n$, computed in $kS_{n-1}$ agree with those in $kS_n$: the primary idempotents in $k[L_r]$ are uniquely determined by their congruences modulo the powers of the distinct linear factors of the minimal polynomial, and the inclusion preserves the polynomial relations of $L_r$. Summing once over each distinct sequence representing $\alpha$ gives $e_{\alpha,\alpha_i}=\sum_{\mathbf j\in I_\alpha}e((\mathbf j,i))=e_\alpha^{(n-1)}P_{n,i}$. By step 4.2 the first factor is central in $kS_{n-1}$, while the second commutes with $kS_{n-1}$ by [F1]; their product is therefore an idempotent commuting with that subgroup algebra. The sets $\{(\mathbf j,i):\mathbf j\in I_\alpha\}$ are disjoint for distinct pairs $(\alpha,i)$ and partition all tuples of length $n$. Consequently these idempotents are pairwise orthogonal and sum to $1$, and their images are $kS_{n-1}$-submodules. Grouping these actual direct summands by the last residue gives $\operatorname{Res}M=\bigoplus_{i\in\mathbb F_p}E_iM$. The indexing is by content classes throughout, so no content is counted twice. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.2, F1, F5, algebra]
''' +s[b:]
s=s.replace('[step 4.1, step 5.1, F5, algebra]','[step 4.2, step 5.1, F5, algebra]').replace('[step 4.1, F4, F6, given]','[step 4.2, F4, F6, given]').replace('For a content vector that is the content of a partition','For a content class that is the content of a partition')
(out/(ids[0]+'.md')).write_text(s)
s=(out/(ids[1]+'.authentic-preimage.md')).read_text().replace('$-\\otimes_{kH}kG$ and induction preserve exactness from the right','$kG\\otimes_{kH}-$, that is induction, preserves exactness').replace('Induction $-\\otimes_{kH}kG$ is right exact','Induction $kG\\otimes_{kH}-$ is right exact')
a=s.index('1.2 The inverse');b=s.index('\n3.1 ',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''1.2 The inverse on coinduced maps. For $\varphi\in\operatorname{Coind}_H^GM$, define $\Psi_M(\varphi)=\sum_{t\in T}t\otimes\varphi(t^{-1})$. This is a finite sum and a $k$-linear map. Inverting the left cosets $tH$ shows that the right cosets $Ht^{-1}$ partition $G$. If a representative is replaced by $t'=th$ with $h\in H$, then $t'\otimes\varphi(t'^{-1})=th\otimes\varphi(h^{-1}t^{-1})=th\otimes h^{-1}\varphi(t^{-1})=t\otimes\varphi(t^{-1})$, by $kH$-linearity and the balancing relation. Thus the inverse formula is independent of the finite transversal. [given, F1, F4]

2.1 $\Phi_M\Psi_M=\mathrm{id}$. For $x\in G$, there is exactly one $t_0\in T$ for which $x\in Ht_0^{-1}$, equivalently $xt_0\in H$. Write $x=ht_0^{-1}$. Then $\Phi_M(\Psi_M(\varphi))(x)=\sum_{t\in T,\ xt\in H}(xt)\varphi(t^{-1})=h\varphi(t_0^{-1})=\varphi(x)$, using the right-coset partition from step 1.2 and $kH$-linearity. Equality on the group basis gives equality on $kG$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F1, F4]

2.2 $\Psi_M\Phi_M=\mathrm{id}$. For $g\in G$ and $m\in M$, write uniquely $g=t_0h$ with $t_0\in T$ and $h\in H$. Then $\Psi_M(\Phi_M(g\otimes m))=\sum_{t\in T,\ t^{-1}g\in H}t\otimes(t^{-1}g)m=t_0\otimes hm=g\otimes m$, by the left-coset partition and tensor balancing. Since such elementary tensors span the induced module, this proves the second inverse identity. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F1, F4]
''' +s[b:]
(out/(ids[1]+'.md')).write_text(s)
for id in ids:guards[id]['proposal_raw_sha256']=hashlib.sha256((out/(id+'.md')).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
(out/'proposal-guards.json').write_text(json.dumps({'version':1,'role':'construction-only; no mathematical acceptance','items':guards},indent=2)+'\n')
